  "body": "This contributes the complete 66-module Lean toolkit for proving physical multitape computations and their costs. It combines the earlier foundations and all subsequent extensions in one self-contained package under `research/multitape-proof-toolkit`. External proof dependencies are Lean/Mathlib; every additional proof module is bundled, with source attribution, so contributors do not need a personal Lean tree.\n\nThe contribution includes:\n\n- Finite machine models with local writes, unit head movement, relative and absolute tape semantics, execution and halting proofs, exact time contracts, and asymptotic cost transport.\n- Verified homogeneous and heterogeneous TM2 compilation, physical simulation overhead, stack encoding/decoding, output recovery, and fixed bottom-anchor theorems relating final heads to final stack lengths.\n- Locality, dirty-frame replay, finite footprints, spectator preservation, terminal observations, sequential composition, finite control embeddings, scheduling, and charged handoffs.\n- Physical stream, parking, marker-installation/removal and restoration routines; arbitrary finite tape-bank embeddings preserve costs, and generic callers allow children to use additional work tapes and return explicit changed workspace. Closed marked and unmarked controllers have exact costs `6|x| + 5|y| + 18` and `6|x| + 5|y| + 24` respectively.\n- Fixed-width and canonical binary output interfaces, including a five-state serializer that converts a padded LSB stack to an MSB word at origin zero in exactly `3m + 4` physical steps, erases the source, restores the specified heads, and preserves spectator tapes.\n- A fixed six-tape unsigned multiplier: for all `a,b < 2^n`, it halts with the exact padded `2n`-bit MSB product at origin zero within `280n\u00b2 + 314n + 117 <= 400(n+1)\u00b2` physical transitions. This includes input loading, output serialization and handoff, with width zero proved.\n- Reused and attributed Problem 1.8 RAM/TM encoding, microcode, arithmetic and polynomial-cost proofs, bundled under public module paths.\n- Pinned dependencies, source manifests, provenance, complete declaration/axiom auditing, rejection controls, replay scripts and an optional formal CI recipe.\n\nValidation: a fresh rebuild of the exact uploaded package passed on Lean 4.34.1 with Mathlib pinned at `d13f23b723b8a846827a245b89c10fc7d3f11612`. The defining-module audit covers 5,028 declarations, including 2,582 theorem declarations and 484 private declarations. No package axioms are admitted; only `propext`, `Classical.choice` and `Quot.sound` are allowed. Finite operational controls and actual Lean negative fixtures also passed. Hosted CI and maintainer review remain separate checks.\n\nThis supplies reusable proved infrastructure for future multiplication algorithms and cost analyses; it does not change kappa. The quadratic example takes two stack-encoded operands, retains explicitly described residual arithmetic workspace, and does not yet provide the serialized-input, alphabet and full-cleanup bridges needed to use it directly as a generic caller child. No fast recursive multiplication or complete cleanup theorem is claimed.",
